# Fieldkit — tenancy + RLS slice

# 1) Start infra (Postgres + Redis from compose.yml)
docker compose up -d
  # Starts:
  # - fieldkit-postgres-1 (Postgres 16 on localhost:5432)
  # - fieldkit-redis-1 (Redis 7 on localhost:6379)

# 2) Create Alembic scaffolding
source .venv/bin/activate
alembic init alembic
  # Creates:
  # - alembic.ini (config, DB URL)
  # - alembic/env.py, script.py.mako, README
  # - alembic/versions/ (empty folder for migrations)

# 3) Point Alembic at local Postgres
# (edited in alembic.ini)
sqlalchemy.url = postgresql+psycopg://fieldkit:fieldkit@localhost:5432/fieldkit
  # Connect as user=fieldkit, password=fieldkit, db=fieldkit on localhost:5432

# 4) Create first migration file
alembic revision -m "create jobs with tenant rls"
  # Generated: alembic/versions/67d09138198a_create_jobs_with_tenant_rls.py

# 5) (Because Alembic login from host kept failing) Apply migration SQL manually inside the container

# 5a) Open psql inside container as fieldkit
docker exec -it fieldkit-postgres-1 psql -U fieldkit
  # psql prompt: fieldkit=#

# 5b) Run the schema + RLS setup once (copied from the migration)
-- 1) jobs table: one row per job, with tenant_id on every row
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS jobs (
    id        uuid PRIMARY KEY,
    tenant_id uuid NOT NULL,
    title     text NOT NULL
);

-- 2) enable and force RLS on jobs
ALTER TABLE jobs ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE jobs FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

-- 3) tenant isolation policy
DROP POLICY IF EXISTS jobs_tenant_isolation ON jobs;
CREATE POLICY jobs_tenant_isolation ON jobs
    USING (tenant_id = current_setting('app.tenant_id', true)::uuid)
    WITH CHECK (tenant_id = current_setting('app.tenant_id', true)::uuid);

-- 4) allow the app role to use the table (we are logged in as fieldkit)
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON jobs TO fieldkit;

# Results:
# - jobs table exists with id, tenant_id, title
# - RLS enabled + forced on jobs
# - jobs_tenant_isolation policy installed
# - fieldkit role can SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE its own rows on jobs

########################################################


# RLS in simple words

jobs.tenant_id is stored on each job row. It says who owns the job.

app.tenant_id is a temporary note on the connection. It says who is asking right now.

set_config writes that note.
current_setting reads that note.

RLS rule:
show a row only if job.tenant_id equals the note app.tenant_id.

Example:
Note says A.
Job A matches -> show it.
Job B does not match -> hide it.

The note is not saved in the table.
It lasts only for this request.

########################################################

# Later notes — jobs, technicians, skills (2026-10-17)

Postgres is Docker on port 5434. The Mac's own Postgres owns 5432.
Data lives on the pgdata disk, not inside the container.
Redis has no disk on purpose. A lock should die on restart.

alembic.ini has the local address:
postgresql+psycopg://fieldkit:fieldkit@localhost:5434/fieldkit
fieldkit / fieldkit is the dummy Docker login. A real password stays out of git.

## Alembic

A migration file is one page. revision is this page's id.
down_revision is the page before it.
Postgres stores only the last finished page id, in alembic_version.
upgrade runs pages after that id. It does not re-read a page whose id is already saved.
downgrade moves the saved id backward. It does not delete the Python file.

Chain so far:
67d09138198a  jobs, app_user, RLS
d7e9ae56e455  technicians
6cef32599cd3  skills and technician_skills

sa describes a column. op sends SQL on the connection env.py opened.
A declarative class (class Technician(Base)) comes later.
It is how the API reads and writes rows. It does not create the table.
create_all is what Permit Tracker used. Fieldkit uses migrations instead.

## app_user

Created once in the jobs migration:
CREATE ROLE app_user LOGIN PASSWORD 'app_user'
The API and the test connect as app_user.
fieldkit owns the tables and is the Docker superuser, so it skips RLS.
app_user does not. Every request must set_config the company id
or the query returns no rows.

## tenant_id

One company, one UUID, created in Python with uuid.uuid4().
That same value is copied onto every row the company owns:
jobs, technicians, skills, technician_skills.
id on a row is that row. tenant_id is the company.
There is no tenants table yet. The columns are not foreign keys to each other.

ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY is the permanent switch.
CREATE POLICY is the permanent match:
tenant_id = current_setting('app.tenant_id', true)::uuid
current_setting reads the note. The note is text.
::uuid turns that text into a UUID so it can be compared to the column.
USING filters rows already stored. WITH CHECK rejects a bad write.
The note is temporary. true on set_config drops it at the end of the transaction.

## Tables

technicians: id, tenant_id, name, email, phone, active.
active defaults to true. A person who leaves stays for old jobs
and can be left out of scoring.

skills: id, tenant_id, name.
Unique (tenant_id, name): one company cannot have two skills with the same name.
Another company can use the same name.
id stays put if the name is edited, so links do not break.

technician_skills: tenant_id, technician_id, skill_id.
No id column. The pair (technician_id, skill_id) is the primary key.
That pair is the whole fact: this person has this skill.
Foreign keys point at technicians.id and skills.id.
A link to a missing person or skill is rejected.

## Unique constraint vs primary key

Both reject a duplicate combination.
Primary key is the row's name. One per table. Cannot be null.
Other tables store it. Changing it breaks those links unless they change too.
Unique constraint is an extra ban beside the primary key.
It does not name the row. Editing the name updates the label.
The id, and every link holding that id, stays.

## Time taken and performance history

The clock is two columns on the job: started_at and completed_at, in UTC.
Minutes for that visit are completed_at minus started_at.
A person does not type the minutes. The two timestamps are the fact.

job_work_log is one row after the visit: what failed, which part was replaced.
That is the story. The clock is the duration.

Do not store a performance number on the technician row.
The API would have to update it on every job, and it would drift.
dbt reads the finished jobs and computes, per company and per technician:
jobs completed, median minutes, on-time rate.
The dashboard shows those dbt numbers.
The assistant can read them with get_metric and describe them.
It does not calculate them.

## Not built yet

shifts, customers, sites, trucks, parts, van stock, audit log.
Then widen jobs: customer, site, skill, status, SLA, assigned tech,
job_type, started_at, completed_at.
Then job_work_log.
Redis assign lock.
Then the engine: score, easy-job auto-assign, exception queue.
The model never assigns a technician and never invents a number.